4.0.5
2026-11-05
- Change (web colour): the dashboard said everything in grey and black, so a problem looked the same as a healthy box. This was clearest in IE on Windows 7. Each status tile is now coloured by its state: green ok, amber waiting or warning, red problem, grey off. Each tile gets a coloured left bar, a tinted background and a coloured value.
  - Engine: red when stopped.
  - Monitors: red if any is down, amber while any is still unchecked.
  - Heartbeat: amber until the first OK reply, red while failing.
  - Reply signing: amber until the server signs its replies.
  - The colour is decided once, in Python (`views.tones()`). It is rendered into the page and sent in `/api/status` as `tones`, and app.js only applies it. The monitor count (`counts`) is now rendered server-side too, so it shows without JavaScript.
  - Also: an accent line on the top bar, the active menu item in the accent colour, a tinted table header, and tinted ok/warn messages.
  - Works in IE9+ (rgba tints) and in both light and dark themes. Checked in Chrome light, dark and with the IE fallback layout.

4.0.4
2026-11-04
- Fix (old browsers): the web UI now works in Internet Explorer 8 to 11, the browsers Windows 7 ships with. On a real Windows 7 32-bit box (IE10) the page looked broken.
  - Every response sends `X-UA-Compatible: IE=edge`. Without it, IE treats localhost and LAN addresses as intranet sites and renders them as IE7.
  - CSS: each `var(--x)` now has a plain colour just before it, which IE uses. Flex, grid and gap moved into `@supports (display: grid)`, which IE skips. IE gets a float/inline-block layout instead.
  - app.js rewritten in ES3: XMLHttpRequest instead of fetch, per-element handlers with an attachEvent fallback, no classList, closest, forEach or textContent-only code. The dashboard rows are rebuilt node by node, because IE9 and older cannot set innerHTML on a tbody. The script moved to the end of body and is no longer `defer`.
  - Templates use `div` with a `role` instead of main/header/nav/footer/section. IE8 cannot style HTML5 elements, and IE9-11 do not know `main`.
  - Modern browsers render as before: checked in Chrome, light and dark, and with the `@supports` block removed to preview what IE gets.
  - New OldBrowserTests guard these rules: var fallbacks, flex/grid only inside @supports, no HTML5 sectioning tags, the X-UA meta, and no ES5+ tokens in app.js. They fail against the 4.0.3 files.

4.0.3
2026-11-04
- New (install.bat): a Windows installer you can double-click. It asks for administrator rights itself (UAC, through PowerShell's Start-Process -Verb RunAs, which Windows 7's PowerShell 2.0 has) and finds the system Python 3.8 or newer (`py -3`, then `python`; the Microsoft Store stub is skipped). It runs install.py with that Python, passing options through, and pauses so the elevated window stays open on the result. The admin check uses `fltmc`, not `net session`, which fails on boxes with the Server service disabled. A `/elevated` marker stops it from asking again in a loop. The file is ASCII with CRLF line endings, enforced by .gitattributes and a unit test. It is shipped in the release zip.

4.0.2
2026-11-04
- Change (always .venv): every agent process now runs on this folder's `.venv` interpreter. That covers the supervisor, engine and web UI, the CLI, and the pip run during an update. The packages from requirements.txt therefore live only in `.venv` and never mix with the system Python's.
  - Started with any other Python (`python main.py status` from a prompt), main.py re-launches itself on `.venv`'s python with the same arguments.
  - With no `.venv`, or with packages missing from it, main.py stops with one line naming the fix: `python install.py`.
  - Autostart refuses to register without `.venv`.
  - install.py, which creates `.venv`, is the only script meant for the system Python.
- Change (pinned dependencies): requirements.txt now pins every package the agent loads, the indirect dependencies too: Jinja2, MarkupSafe, click, blinker, itsdangerous, idna, charset-normalizer, importlib-metadata, zipp. Before, they were resolved on the day of install, so two boxes could differ. MarkupSafe is pinned per Python version: 2.1.5 below 3.9, 3.0.4 from 3.9. Verified with fresh `install.py` runs on Python 3.8 and 3.14 plus `pip check`.

4.0.1
2026-11-04
- Security (file permissions): data/ holds the HMAC secret, the api_key, push tokens and password hashes, and logs/ holds the agent's history. Until now, on Windows, both folders took their permissions from the parent folder. Under C:\ that means every local user could read them. On Linux and macOS, a file made by hand (`cp config/env.example data/.env`) or restored from a backup kept mode 0644. The supervisor now re-applies owner-only permissions on every start (new `uptime_sync/perms.py`).
  - Windows: icacls removes inheritance. It grants full control to SYSTEM and Administrators, by well-known SID so localized Windows works the same, and to the user who ran `main.py install`. Everything inside then inherits exactly that.
  - POSIX: folders become 0700 and files 0600, and every agent process runs with umask 077.
  - A failure is logged as a [SECURITY] warning and never stops the agent.
  - `main.py status` lists anything other local users can still read. `main.py install` applies the permissions immediately.
- Fix (sudo installs): `sudo main.py install` / `start` / `import` / `reset-password` left root-owned files in data/, logs/ and run/. The systemd unit or LaunchDaemon runs the agent as the sudo user, so it could not write its logs, and with 0600 it could not read its own settings either. These commands now hand those folders back to the sudo user before the service starts and when they finish.

